<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<RunSettings>

  <RunConfiguration>
    <!--
      WHY THIS EXISTS. Four test classes inject real Windows pointer and keyboard messages.
      They prove the one thing nothing else can: that input actually travels the whole path,
      from the window through the input site and the hosted ListView into the gesture arbiter.
      Every other test in this project calls our code directly, so all 201 of them would still
      pass if the arbiter were never attached to the ListView at all.

      The cost is that injection takes the pointer and keyboard away from whoever is sitting at
      the machine, for the length of the run. And the path they cover only changes when input
      code changes, so paying that on every build buys nothing.

      So they are excluded here, and asked for deliberately, with the test filter
      TestCategory=Interactive on the dotnet test command line. (The switch itself cannot be
      written here: an XML comment may not contain two hyphens in a row.)

      Run them after touching input handling, a template part name, or an event subscription —
      the three ways that path breaks silently.
    -->
    <TestCaseFilter>TestCategory!=Interactive</TestCaseFilter>
  </RunConfiguration>

</RunSettings>
